ome merits that I can't think of right now, but here's a list of things that she should have, anyway.\n\nI had more to say. I can't remember what it was. Maybe I'll open the form up again later tonight, but i'll submit this now in case I don't get that far."},"updated_at":"2026-04-17T03:34:29.069Z","feeding_review":{"pool_player":"Undefined","pool_validated":"Wits 3 + Empathy 3 + 1 = 7","pool_status":"validated","notes_thread":[],"story_context":"","pool_mod_equipment":1,"vitae_rite_cost":1,"description":"Anichka is discerning with who she feeds from, looking for targets who haven't been drinking heavily or doing drugs (she'll branch out to 'not doing hard drugs' if finding someone with no drugs proves too hard). If it looks particularly sparse, she'll start using Auspex to try and find someone suitable, but not otherwise. Basically just trying to feed sensibly.","blood_type":"Human","nine_again":false,"eight_again":false,"pool_validated_by":"Angelus","pool_committed_by":"Angelus"},"merit_actions_resolved":[{"pool_player":"","pool_validated":"","pool_status":"resolved","notes_thread":[{"author_id":"405594065841946624","author_name":"Von Vagabond","text":"They do as they are told and she gets a very nice shovel.","created_at":"2026-04-14T09:16:15.075Z"}],"story_context":"","action_type_override":"acquisition","desired_outcome":"","description":"The family should continue living and establishing themselves, making themselves at home and part of the community, generally building their own safe space, with access to their culture for themselves and the children.\n\nIt is Orthodox Easter on the weekend of the 12th. Anichka urges her family to go if they're feeling it, especially if it will help create ties among the community and all that good shit.\n\nShe also asks them to get her a shovel, please, one for heavy duty use.","pool_resolved_by":"Angelus","merit_outcome":"approved"}],"st_review":{"territory_overrides":{"feeding":["academy"]},"travel_discretion":"subtle","outcome_text":"## Letter from Home\n\nChild,\nHalyna is well, and she is also teaching the spring rites to the younger ones in a way that would make you wince if you could see it. She learned from your notes rather than from mine, which I know because she makes the same mistake with the third invocation that you used to make, and I have chosen not to correct her because watching you both get it wrong in exactly the same way has become one of the small pleasures of my evenings.\nThe coven stands. The roof still leaks in the same corner. I mention this because you asked if they were well, and the answer is that they are exactly as they were when you left, which is the most honest measure of wellness I can offer.\nYour garden will take time. The soil in that country does not know our blood yet, and it will refuse you before it accepts you, and then it will refuse you again because it has forgotten that it already accepted you. This is not a failing on your part. It is how soil works when it has not been spoken to in eighty years. Cut your hands on it as many times as necessary. The plants do not care about your comfort; they care about your persistence.\nYou write about the angry lady in the ocean and the old temple and the borders in the same paragraph, as though they are all one problem. They are not. You know this, because you separated them in your next sentence, but I want to be clear: walk the borders first, tend the garden second, and leave the dreaming until your feet are planted somewhere solid. The old temple has waited this long and it will wait for you.\nYour Maiden Alice sounds young and recently burdened. Do not let her carry weight she has not been taught to hold. Teach her the practical things before you teach her the sacred ones, because the practical things are what will keep her standing when the sacred ones become heavy.\nI am where I have always been. The roof leaks. The coven persists. Write when you can, and write longer when you do.\n\n## Touchstone\n\nKateryna cannot sleep and has come to find you about it, carrying the blanket from her bed in a bundle against her chest as though it were something she had rescued. She stands in the doorway and announces this fact with the grave authority of a child who considers sleeplessness a serious injustice that someone ought to address.\nYou are sitting on the floor with a half-finished piece of embroidery across your knee, and you move it aside without comment to make room for her. She settles next to you, arranges the blanket into a nest around her legs, and watches your hands work the needle for a while with the particular intensity of a child who is pretending not to be tired.\nShe asks what you are making. You tell her it is a ribbon for a friend. She asks if the friend is nice, and you say yes, and she asks if the friend is here in Sydney, and you say yes, and she asks if Sydney is going to be home now, and you do not answer that one immediately because the question is larger than she knows.\nYou thread a new colour instead, a red that catches the lamplight, and she leans against your arm with the full unthinking weight of a child who has decided you are furniture. Her breathing slows. The embroidery takes shape under your hands: a pattern your grandmother taught you, carried across borders and decades and one particular kind of death, and now stitched into a ribbon in a rented house in a country where the soil does not yet know your name.\nKateryna is asleep before you finish the first repeat of the pattern. You do not move your arm.\n\n## The Barrens\n\nThe land around Ku-ring-gai does not belong to you and it does not pretend to. You have lived here for two months now and the bush has accepted your presence the way it accepts anything that does not try to change it: with indifference and the constant quiet reminder that you are a guest in something older than your kind.\nThe garden is taking root slowly, and the soil resists you exactly the way your sire said it would. You have cut your hands on the local plants often enough that you have stopped noticing the sting, and the first shoots of what you planted last month have survived, which is not the same as thriving but is enough to keep going.\nThe strange figures you glimpsed last month on the paths toward Pittwater are still there, or others like them. You catch the edges of their movement on two occasions this month: once at dusk near the fire trail above McCarrs Creek, once on the road down to Church Point. They move through the bush with a sureness that is not human and not Kindred and not animal, and they are very good at not being where you are looking by the time you look. You have decided not to follow them yet, because the land they are moving towards does not feel like it wants you to.\nThe blood this month came from the rave in the Academy rather than from the bush, which was a relief your body registered even if you did not intend to acknowledge it. Animal blood sustains, but it does not satisfy, and the difference between the two is something you are learning to carry without complaint.\n\n## Patrolling the Academy\n\nYou walk the Academy across four nights and what you come back with is the feeling more than the detail. Jack is out on the territory and you know this because the territory carries the quality of being attended to. One other patroller crosses your path near Victoria Park, a brief mutual acknowledgement and nothing more. The Beast's Hackles prickle twice on King Street where the occult shops have been busier than they should be, and once near a laneway behind Enmore Theatre where somebody has been standing for a long time without moving. You file the impressions and leave the analysis to those who patrol with more than instinct.\n\n## Patrolling the Shore\n\nThe North Shore is less familiar ground and your patrol reads it that way, in fragments rather than in the full picture. Alice is on the territory and you feel her the way you feel any Regent walking her own ground, a settled presence that tells you the territory is already watched. Brandy you catch once on Military Road, striding with the confidence of somebody who wants to be seen. The foot traffic is healthy and late and the blood would come up clean if you were feeding here. Two faces you do not place pass you near Cremorne Point, moving with the disinterest of people who are very interested in not being interesting.\n\n## Party Party Party\n\nYour part of the ritual is the oldest part and the quietest. While Cyrus holds the beat and Jack seeds the crowd and Keeper works the gantry above, you move through the room at floor level with your hands open and your Cruac running low and steady. What you are doing is not visible. It sits underneath everything else. You are weaving predatory confidence into the feeding, stitching a safety net under the bacchanal so that every Crone who reaches for blood tonight reaches with certainty. The Divination in you reads the room as it tips, each feeder's moment arriving a half-beat before they know it will, and you hold the pattern until the last of them has taken what they need. Four successes. The net holds cleanly. Nobody frenzies who should not frenzy. Nobody feeds who should not feed. Your contribution is invisible and that is exactly how you prefer it.\n\n## For the Others\n\nYou gather the honey yourself from a native bee colony in a park near the Academy, which takes the better part of a night and most of your patience. The shawl is one of your second-tier pieces, still good, still carrying the work of your hands. The pearls you add because the gesture needs weight. The kangaroo vine with fruit still on it you cut from a fence-line in Stanmore, the leaves glossy and dark. You place the offering near where you saw them last, arranged on a flat stone with the deliberateness of somebody who knows how to make an offering and who is choosing, very carefully, not to presume she knows who she is offering to. You come back twice a week across the month. The first visit, nothing has changed. The second, the honey is gone. The shawl has been moved. The pearls remain. On the third visit the vine has been replaced with a different cutting, one you did not plant, its leaves darker than any kangaroo vine you have seen in this city. Somebody has accepted the opening. Somebody has replied.\n\n## Retainer\n\nYour people do as they are told. A very nice shovel arrives by the end of the week, the handle wrapped in soft leather, the blade practical and sturdy. You will be putting it to use before long.","outcome_visibility":"published","published_at":"2026-04-17T08:16:45.334Z"},"st_narrative":{"letter_from_home":{"response":"Child,\nHalyna is well, and she is also teaching the spring rites to the younger ones in a way that would make you wince if you could see it. She learned from your notes rather than from mine, which I know because she makes the same mistake with the third invocation that you used to make, and I have chosen not to correct her because watching you both get it wrong in exactly the same way has become one of the small pleasures of my evenings.\nThe coven stands. The roof still leaks in the same corner. I mention this because you asked if they were well, and the answer is that they are exactly as they were when you left, which is the most honest measure of wellness I can offer.\nYour garden will take time. The soil in that country does not know our blood yet, and it will refuse you before it accepts you, and then it will refuse you again because it has forgotten that it already accepted you. This is not a failing on your part. It is how soil works when it has not been spoken to in eighty years. Cut your hands on it as many times as necessary. The plants do not care about your comfort; they care about your persistence.\nYou write about the angry lady in the ocean and the old temple and the borders in the same paragraph, as though they are all one problem. They are not. You know this, because you separated them in your next sentence, but I want to be clear: walk the borders first, tend the garden second, and leave the dreaming until your feet are planted somewhere solid. The old temple has waited this long and it will wait for you.\nYour Maiden Alice sounds young and recently burdened. Do not let her carry weight she has not been taught to hold. Teach her the practical things before you teach her the sacred ones, because the practical things are what will keep her standing when the sacred ones become heavy.\nI am where I have always been. The roof leaks. The coven persists. Write when you can, and write longer when you do.","author":"alchymiste","status":"complete","revision_note":""},"touchstone":{"response":"Kateryna cannot sleep and has come to find you about it, carrying the blanket from her bed in a bundle against her chest as though it were something she had rescued. She stands in the doorway and announces this fact with the grave authority of a child who considers sleeplessness a serious injustice that someone ought to address.\nYou are sitting on the floor with a half-finished piece of embroidery across your knee, and you move it aside without comment to make room for her. She settles next to you, arranges the blanket into a nest around her legs, and watches your hands work the needle for a while with the particular intensity of a child who is pretending not to be tired.\nShe asks what you are making. You tell her it is a ribbon for a friend. She asks if the friend is nice, and you say yes, and she asks if the friend is here in Sydney, and you say yes, and she asks if Sydney is going to be home now, and you do not answer that one immediately because the question is larger than she knows.\nYou thread a new colour instead, a red that catches the lamplight, and she leans against your arm with the full unthinking weight of a child who has decided you are furniture. Her breathing slows. The embroidery takes shape under your hands: a pattern your grandmother taught you, carried across borders and decades and one particular kind of death, and now stitched into a ribbon in a rented house in a country where the soil does not yet know your name.\nKateryna is asleep before you finish the first repeat of the pattern. You do not move your arm.","author":"alchymiste","status":"complete","revision_note":""},"territory_reports":[{"territory_id":"barrens","response":"The land around Ku-ring-gai does not belong to you and it does not pretend to. You have lived here for two months now and the bush has accepted your presence the way it accepts anything that does not try to change it: with indifference and the constant quiet reminder that you are a guest in something older than your kind.\nThe garden is taking root slowly, and the soil resists you exactly the way your sire said it would. You have cut your hands on the local plants often enough that you have stopped noticing the sting, and the first shoots of what you planted last month have survived, which is not the same as thriving but is enough to keep going.\nThe strange figures you glimpsed last month on the paths toward Pittwater are still there, or others like them. You catch the edges of their movement on two occasions this month: once at dusk near the fire trail above McCarrs Creek, once on the road down to Church Point. They move through the bush with a sureness that is not human and not Kindred and not animal, and they are very good at not being where you are looking by the time you look. You have decided not to follow them yet, because the land they are moving towards does not feel like it wants you to.\nThe blood this month came from the rave in the Academy rather than from the bush, which was a relief your body registered even if you did not intend to acknowledge it. Animal blood sustains, but it does not satisfy, and the difference between the two is something you are learning to carry without complaint.","author":"alchymiste","status":"complete","revision_note":"Seems Keeper and Carver are also possibly co-resident? This seems to be at least slightly broken, not sure it matters enough to wrry about right now."}],"action_responses":[{"action_index":0,"response":"Your people do as they are told. A very nice shovel arrives by the end of the week, the handle wrapped in soft leather, the blade practical and sturdy. You will be putting it to use before long.","author":"Von Vagabond","status":"complete","revision_note":""}],"locked":true,"project_responses":[{"project_index":0,"response":"You walk the Academy across four nights and what you come back with is the feeling more than the detail. Jack is out on the territory and you know this because the territory carries the quality of being attended to. One other patroller crosses your path near Victoria Park, a brief mutual acknowledgement and nothing more. The Beast's Hackles prickle twice on King Street where the occult shops have been busier than they should be, and once near a laneway behind Enmore Theatre where somebody has been standing for a long time without moving. You file the impressions and leave the analysis to those who patrol with more than instinct.","author":"Von Vagabond","status":"complete","revision_note":""},{"project_index":1,"response":"The North Shore is less familiar ground and your patrol reads it that way, in fragments rather than in the full picture. Alice is on the territory and you feel her the way you feel any Regent walking her own ground, a settled presence that tells you the territory is already watched. Brandy you catch once on Military Road, striding with the confidence of somebody who wants to be seen. The foot traffic is healthy and late and the blood would come up clean if you were feeding here. Two faces you do not place pass you near Cremorne Point, moving with the disinterest of people who are very interested in not being interesting.","author":"Von Vagabond","status":"complete","revision_note":""},{"project_index":2,